{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1065 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "83a41c9148a56ab78f5a824cae4dec2f35fc06d9fc2b8cd4713dcf939ea92731" :edb/db-before-t 1064 :edb/db-after-t 1065 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534377 50 #inst "2026-10-06T18:30:51.836Z" 13194139534377 true] [17592186091972 1100 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+5y-2z=-2 \\\\ -3y+z=-2 \\\\ 3x-7y+3z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Given that $\\mathbf x = \\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534377 true] [17592186091972 1100 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n${-2x + 5y - 2z =-2; - 3y + z =-2; 3x - 7y + 3z = 3$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$. Given that $\\mathbf x = \\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z$?\n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534377 false] [17592186091972 1145 "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAx &= b \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= A^{-1}b \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is\n\n$$\nx=3, y=0, z=-2\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx+y+z=3+0+(-2)=1\n$$" 13194139534377 false] [17592186091972 1145 "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is given by $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=3, y=0, z=-2.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=3+0+(-2)=1.$" 13194139534377 true] [17592186091973 1100 "Solve the following system of linear equations using matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3y+x+z=0 \\\\ z+4y-5=2 \\\\ 1+2x-y=-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534377 true] [17592186091973 1100 "Solve the following system of linear equations using matrices:\n${3y + x + z = 0; z + 4y - 5 = 2; 1 + 2x - y =-3$" 13194139534377 false] [17592186091973 1145 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are in the correct order and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+3y+z=0 \\\\ 0x+4y+z=7 \\\\ 2x-y+0z=-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is given by $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we compute $A^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1*: Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1)-3(-2)+(-8) \\\\  & =1+6-8 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2*: Compute the matrix of minors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3*:  Find the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Step 4*: Transpose the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Step 5*: Apply the inverse matrix formula.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate the solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -33\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3,$ $y=10,$ $z=-33.$" 13194139534377 true] [17592186091973 1145 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are in the correct order and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n{x + 3y + z = 0; 0x + 4y + z = 7; 2x - y + 0z =-4\n$$\n\nNext, we write the system in the form\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute $A^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1*: Find the determinant of $A$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} &= 1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}- 3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+ 1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (1) - 3(-2) + (-8) \\\\\n&= 1 + 6 - 8 \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2*: Compute the matrix of minors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= [\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid ] \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3*: Find the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Step 4*: Transpose the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Step 5*: Apply the inverse matrix formula.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate the solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= A^{-1}b \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -33\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3$, $y=10$, $z=-33$." 13194139534377 false] [17592186091974 1100 "Consider a matrix equation $B\\,\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\,\\mathbf{d}$, where $A$ and $B$ are $10 \\times 10$ non-singular matrices, while $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are $10 \\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $\\mathbf{x}$." 13194139534377 false] [17592186091974 1100 "Consider a matrix equation $B\\,\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\,\\mathbf{d},$ where $A$ and $B$ are $10 \\times 10$ non-singular matrices, while $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are $10 \\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $\\mathbf{x}.$" 13194139534377 true] [17592186091974 1145 "Since $B$ is not singular, it must have an inverse $B^{-1}$. Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation\n\n$$\nB\\,\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\,\\mathbf{d}\n$$\n\nby $B^{-1}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBx &= A^{-1}d \\\\\nB^{-1}Bx &= B^{-1}A^{-1}d \\\\\nI_{10}x &= B^{-1}A^{-1}d \\\\\nx &= B^{-1}A^{-1}d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534377 false] [17592186091974 1145 "Since $B$ is not singular, it must have an inverse $B^{−1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $B\\,\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\,\\mathbf{d}$ by $B^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {B}^{-1}B\\,x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{10}\\,x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534377 true] [17592186092132 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284859 13194139534377 true] [17592186092132 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284874 13194139534377 true] [17592186092132 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284881 13194139534377 true] [17592186092132 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284888 13194139534377 true] [17592186092133 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284845 13194139534377 true] [17592186092133 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284852 13194139534377 true] [17592186092133 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284866 13194139534377 true] [17592186092134 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284895 13194139534377 true] [17592186092134 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284902 13194139534377 true] [17592186092134 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284909 13194139534377 true] [17592186092134 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284916 13194139534377 true] [17592186092656 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534377 true] [17592186092656 1100 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-3+z=0 \\\\ 5y-1+3x=2z+1 \\\\ 3z+2x-1=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind  $x+y+z.$" 13194139534377 true] [17592186092656 1100 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n${2x - 3 + z = 0; 5y - 1 + 3x = 2z + 1; 3z + 2x - 1 = 2y$\nFind $x + y + z$." 13194139534377 false] [17592186092656 1145 "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+z=3 \\\\ 3x+5y-2z=2 \\\\ 2x-2y+3z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-0+|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(15-4)+(-6-10) \\\\  & =22-16 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 13 & -16 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -13 & -16 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=4-4-5=-5.$" 13194139534377 true] [17592186092759 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534377 true] [17592186092759 1100 "Consider a system of $3$ linear equations in $3$ variables, written in matrix form as $D\\,y=w.$ Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $y$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $w$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,y & =w \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,y & =w \\\\ {I}_{3}\\,y & ={D}^{-1}\\,w \\\\  & ={D}^{-1}\\,w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom and left to right, what are the missing parts of the derivation?" 13194139534377 true] [17592186092759 1100 "Consider a system of $3$ linear equations in $3$ variables, written in matrix form as $Dy = w$. Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $y$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $w$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$Dy|= w; D^{-1}Dy|= D - 1w; I_{3}y|= D^{-1}w; D|= D^{-1}w$\n\nFrom top to bottom and left to right, what are the missing parts of the derivation?" 13194139534377 false] [17592186092759 1145 "Since $D$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${D}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $D\\,y=w$ by ${D}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,y & =w \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,y & ={D}^{-1}w \\\\ {I}_{3}\\,y & ={D}^{-1}\\,w \\\\ y & ={D}^{-1}\\,w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are ${D}^{-1}$ and $y.$" 13194139534377 true] [17592186092764 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534377 true] [17592186092764 1100 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+3z=2 \\\\ x-2y-5z=-1 \\\\ 2x-3y+2z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-19 & -7 & 11 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534377 true] [17592186092764 1100 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n${x - y + 3z = 2; x - 2y - 5z =-1; 2x - 3y + 2z = 1$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax = b$. Given that $x = ⟨x, y, z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x + y + z$?\n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n$A^{-1} =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-19 & -7 & 11 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139534377 false] [17592186092764 1145 "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]